    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "This is Liam, in for Bear. A quantum computer does not win by trying every answer and reading them all. Measurement destroys that fantasy. The useful resource is interference.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Explain quantum speedup without the parallel-universes myth.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — ALGORITHMS",
            "segment": "Speed from Interference",
            "greeting": "Dia dhuit, Liam",
            "runningText": "separating superposition from speedup…",
            "output": [
              "Superposition creates amplitudes, not readable answer lists.",
              "Interference reshapes the final probability pattern."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Start with a register in superposition. Its state assigns an amplitude to many computational-basis inputs. That is one quantum state, not a shelf of classical processors.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "A reversible oracle can act on all those basis components coherently. People call this quantum parallelism, but by itself it has not delivered a speedup.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Measure immediately and you receive one ordinary outcome, sampled according to squared amplitude. You do not get a printout of every function value.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Repeat that naive strategy and it behaves like random sampling. Many populated branches are useless if the answer remains hidden in inaccessible amplitudes.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The circuit must instead arrange phases. Amplitudes are arrows: direction carries phase, and arrows leading to the same output add before probabilities are calculated.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "drawon"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "When equal arrows point opposite ways, they cancel. That is destructive interference: a wrong output can be driven toward zero probability.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "When arrows align, they reinforce. Constructive interference raises the amplitude of outputs that encode the desired global structure.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Now measurement becomes useful. The circuit has reshaped the distribution so a revealing outcome is likely, sometimes certain in an idealized algorithm.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The noise-cancelling-headphones analogy is close: do not inspect every sound wave separately. Engineer a second wave so unwanted patterns cancel and useful structure survives.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "But interference is not automatically an advantage. A useful algorithm needs a problem structure, a circuit that encodes it into relative phase, and a measurement that exposes the result.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "threshold"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B11.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "So the correction is precise: superposition lets one evolution carry many amplitudes. Interference filters their global pattern. Measurement extracts one carefully biased result.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B12.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B12.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B13",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Your turn. If a circuit creates a huge superposition and measures it immediately, what information does it return, and what essential step is missing? Pause before the answer: one random basis outcome; interference is missing.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Your turn: diagnose the missing step.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — CHECK",
            "segment": "Superposition Is Not Enough",
            "greeting": "Over to you, Liam",
            "runningText": "waiting for your explanation…",
            "output": [
              "Immediate measurement: one sampled basis outcome.",
              "Missing resource: controlled interference."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B13.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B13.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B13.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T22:47:01"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B14",
      "estimated_duration_s": 7,
      "narration_text": "Quantum speed is not trying everything at once. It is engineering interference so one measurement says something global.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "fade"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B14.mp3",
